KiRaRay native OpenPBR subset

The lookup tables and shading formulas are adapted from Adobe's OpenPBR BSDF
reference implementation, revision c91aad1d1ce1693e803f039d7c92c2965c4eb013:
https://github.com/adobe/openpbr-bsdf
Copyright 2026 Adobe. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (LICENSE).

The LTC fuzz formulas and table originate from "Practical Multiple-Scattering
Sheen Using Linearly Transformed Cosines", Tizian Zeltner, Brent Burley, and
Matt Jen-Yuan Chiang (2022), https://github.com/tizian/ltc-sheen, Apache-2.0.

KiRaRay uses native sampled spectra for color coefficients and its own lobe
sampling mixture. Subsurface, volumes, dispersion, thin film and coat anisotropy
are excluded. Roughness has the reference implementation's 0.001 minimum:
zero roughness remains a finite glossy lobe, with continuous PDF/MIS flags.
Refraction IOR is kept at least 0.0001 away from unity, as in the reference,
to avoid a degenerate transmission half-vector; legacy BSDF delta behavior
is unchanged.
OpenPBR emission_luminance is measured in nits. KiRaRay defines 1000 nits as
one scene-linear radiance unit. Blender 5.2.2 exports Principled emission
strength directly, so the Blender adapter and validated USD metadata explicitly
scale that expression by 1000 before native material compilation. Ordinary
unannotated USD retains its authored nits; no producer heuristic rescales it.
USD Preview Surface and legacy emission retain their existing radiance units.
For rough thin sheets, KiRaRay compensates the flipped GGX reflection and
transmission with the ideal-metal energy complement table weighted by the
window's R/T coefficients. This intentionally replaces the reference's
refractive dielectric compensation on that path, preserving a nonabsorbing
rough sheet's white-furnace energy and the same smooth-window limit.
The default reference view-dependent coat/fuzz approximation is retained;
this is not a complete OpenPBR conformance implementation or a Cycles match.
